{"name":"Grand Canyon","hex":"#a05d4d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Grand Canyon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A large national park and gorge carved by the Colorado River, located in Arizona, United States.","Synonym of Grand Canyon Village, a settlement on the south rim of the canyon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Grand_Canyon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grand Canyon","blocks":[{"text":"The Grand Canyon is a steep-sided canyon carved by the Colorado River in Arizona, United States. The Grand Canyon is 277 miles (446 km) long, up to 18 miles (29 km) wide and attains a depth of over a mile."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Canyon_River_Tree_%28165872763%29.jpeg/330px-Canyon_River_Tree_%28165872763%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Canyon","wikidata":"Q118841"},{"title":"Grand Canyon (1991 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Grand Canyon is a 1991 American drama film directed and produced by Lawrence Kasdan, and written by Kasdan with his wife Meg. Featuring an ensemble cast, the film is about random events affecting a diverse group of people, exploring the race- and class-imposed chasms which separate members of the same community."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5e/Grand_canyon_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Canyon_(1991_film)","wikidata":"Q1542548"},{"title":"Canyon","blocks":[{"text":"A canyon, gorge or chasm, is a deep cleft between escarpments or cliffs resulting from weathering and the erosive activity of a river over geologic time scales. Rivers have a natural tendency to cut through underlying surfaces, eventually wearing away rock layers as sediments are removed downstream. A river bed will gradually reach a baseline elevation, which is the same elevation as the body of water into which the river drains. The processes of weathering and erosion will form canyons when the river's headwaters and estuary are at significantly different elevations, particularly through regions where softer rock layers are intermingled with harder layers more resistant to weathering."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg/330px-Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon","wikidata":"Q150784"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/GrandCanyon.NASA.2014.jpg/500px-GrandCanyon.NASA.2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/GrandCanyon.NASA.2014.jpg/1280px-GrandCanyon.NASA.2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/GrandCanyon.NASA.2014.jpg/500px-GrandCanyon.NASA.2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Image of the Grand Canyon and surrounding area taken from the International Space Station","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrandCanyon.NASA.2014.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Grand_Canyon_from_air.JPG/500px-Grand_Canyon_from_air.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Grand_Canyon_from_air.JPG/1280px-Grand_Canyon_from_air.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Grand_Canyon_from_air.JPG/500px-Grand_Canyon_from_air.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Grand Canyon from an airplane, with the Colorado River visible","li